From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000005 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anton <fourth@devpins.org>
Date: Thu, 4 Jun 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sdm845-xiaomi-beryllium: self-power USB host VBUS

Patch 0002 puts &usb_1_dwc3 in dual-role (host-first), but that alone leaves
the USB-C port electrically dead in host mode: dwc3-qcom only asserts the
UTMI_OTG_VBUS_VALID *signal* and never drives 5 V, and mainline has no PMI8998
OTG-boost regulator. A self-powered USB device (Chord Mojo 2) only asserts its
D+ pull-up once it sees real VBUS, so with a plain passive OTG cable nothing
enumerated unless 5 V was fed onto the bus from outside the phone (a powered
Y-cable, or the Mojo's own power-output mode). The downstream Android kernel
"just works" because its qpnp-smb2 driver turns on the PMI8998 OTG boost.

Add the PMI8998 USB VBUS boost regulator at the DCDC peripheral base 0x1100
(under pmi8998_lsid0, the same SPMI LSID as charger@1000, bound independently),
driven by the qcom,pmi8998-vbus-reg support in patch 0004 (needs
CONFIG_REGULATOR_QCOM_USB_VBUS=y).

beryllium has no working Type-C controller / usb-connector graph, so even with
the dwc3 role-switch there is no in-kernel consumer to enable VBUS on host and
disable it on device. Rather than pin it regulator-always-on (which can never be
turned off -- wrong for a port that must also charge), keep the boost a plain
regulator and add a regulator-output userspace-consumer node as its sole,
exclusive consumer (needs CONFIG_REGULATOR_USERSPACE_CONSUMER=y). That exposes
/sys/.../state, so the hifi-usb-mode switch drives VBUS from userspace: enabled
for Audio (host), disabled for Charging / Sync (device) -- it MUST be off in
device mode or it would fight the wall charger's 5 V. VBUS is off at boot until
hifi-player-audio-setup enables it; the DAC then enumerates just as it does after
a resume.

Warning: with the phone sourcing VBUS, do NOT also enable the Mojo 2's own
power-output/charging mode -- two 5 V sources must not fight on the bus.

Signed-off-by: Anton <fourth@devpins.org>
---
 .../qcom/sdm845-xiaomi-beryllium-common.dtsi  | 30 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm845-xiaomi-beryllium-common.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm845-xiaomi-beryllium-common.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm845-xiaomi-beryllium-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm845-xiaomi-beryllium-common.dtsi
@@ -944,3 +944,33 @@
 &wcd9340 { status = "disabled"; };
 &slim { status = "disabled"; };
 &slimbam { status = "disabled"; };
+
+/* Self-power USB VBUS for host mode (PMI8998 OTG boost), made runtime-switchable.
+ * The boost (qcom,pmi8998-vbus-reg, patch 0004 + CONFIG_REGULATOR_QCOM_USB_VBUS=y)
+ * is a PLAIN regulator -- deliberately NOT regulator-always-on -- so it can actually
+ * be turned off at runtime (an always-on rail can't be disabled, and merely unbinding
+ * the driver leaves the OTG_EN bit set). Its sole, exclusive consumer is a
+ * regulator-output userspace node (CONFIG_REGULATOR_USERSPACE_CONSUMER) that exposes
+ * /sys/.../state, so userspace flips VBUS at runtime: the hifi-usb-mode switch enables
+ * it for Audio (host) and disables it for Charging / Sync (device) -- the boost MUST be
+ * off in device mode or it would fight the wall charger. VBUS is off at boot until
+ * hifi-player-audio-setup enables it (the DAC then enumerates, as it does on resume);
+ * beryllium has no Type-C controller / usb-connector to do this in-kernel. Don't also
+ * enable the Mojo 2's own power output -- two 5 V sources must not fight on the bus. */
+&pmi8998_lsid0 {
+	pmi8998_vbus: usb-vbus-regulator@1100 {
+		compatible = "qcom,pmi8998-vbus-reg";
+		reg = <0x1100>;
+		regulator-min-microamp = <500000>;
+		regulator-max-microamp = <1000000>;
+	};
+};
+
+/ {
+	/* Userspace on/off control for the OTG VBUS boost above (driven by hifi-usb-mode
+	 * via /sys/.../state). regulator-output takes the supply exclusively. */
+	usb_vbus_output: usb-vbus-output {
+		compatible = "regulator-output";
+		vout-supply = <&pmi8998_vbus>;
+	};
+};
-- 
